<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Władysław Siciński</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. <a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Władysław Siciński was a deputy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. In 1652 he dissolved the entire Sejm by his solitary objection, the first successful exercise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberum-veto">liberum veto</a>. Accounts of the Commonwealth describe this as establishing a destructive precedent that was later exploited to paralyse the legislature, contributing to the state's governmental weakness.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-sicinski.jpg" alt="Władysław Siciński" width="255" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wladyslaw_Sicinski.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
